1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,912
What happens when the processing center goes completely silent?

2
00:00:03,962 --> 00:00:10,800
A debit with no answer is unknown: the money may have moved, so it is never declined, and never sent again.

3
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:16,464
Someone has to ask the scheme later, and keep asking, even if the app restarts in between.

4
00:00:16,514 --> 00:00:22,128
So each unknown debit gets a Temporal workflow, and Temporal keeps its waits durable.

5
00:00:22,178 --> 00:00:31,176
The workflow only asks for the debit's status, and waits longer each time: one second, then two, four, up to a minute, eight times at most.

6
00:00:31,226 --> 00:00:37,944
It asks with the same reference the debit carried, our payment id, so the answer is about this debit and nothing else.

7
00:00:37,994 --> 00:00:44,112
A Humo hold still waiting is captured as its own step, and only then is the payment paid.

8
00:00:44,162 --> 00:00:49,800
Found makes the payment paid, not found makes it declined, and the journal records the answer.

9
00:00:49,850 --> 00:00:56,232
Not found counts only after the debit call's time limit, because until then the scheme may simply not have it yet.

10
00:00:56,282 --> 00:01:02,184
Found after the buyer's page has closed, the order is over, so the money goes back with a reversal.

11
00:01:02,234 --> 00:01:09,024
The checks always end before the scheme's next clearing cut-off, less a margin, while a reversal is still possible.

12
00:01:09,074 --> 00:01:16,344
Still no answer, and a person decides: the payment goes to manual review, and the merchant hears nothing yet.

13
00:01:16,394 --> 00:01:24,792
In the bank's console, staff see every payment left without an answer, check it with the scheme, and record debited or not, with a required note.

14
00:01:24,842 --> 00:01:35,184
Their decision follows the same rule: not debited is declined, debited is paid, or given back if the page has closed, and each one is audited with who made it.

15
00:01:35,234 --> 00:01:40,272
The workflow's id is the payment's, so starting it twice keeps the first.

16
00:01:40,322 --> 00:01:46,800
A sweep every minute starts it for anything left unknown, and for debits the app stopped in the middle of.

17
00:01:46,850 --> 00:01:53,664
If Temporal itself is down, the debit still ends unknown, and the next sweep starts the workflow.

18
00:01:53,714 --> 00:01:59,688
When the page stops waiting, the payment records that the buyer is owed the result by SMS.

19
00:01:59,738 --> 00:02:02,952
The dashboard counts how unknown debits ended.

20
00:02:03,002 --> 00:02:11,040
A gauge counts the payments still waiting, unknown or in manual review, and an alert emails when they wait longer than the configured time.

21
00:02:11,090 --> 00:02:16,080
Next, telling the merchant what happened, reliably: the outbox and Kafka.
